  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAnother warm day on Thursday, but it will also be accompanied by \nincreased winds due to the next approaching weather system off the \nsouthern CA coast.  Cross barrier flow will pick back up and another \nlee surface low is progged to set up in southeast CO, tightening up \nsurface gradients so that south winds will be back in that 10-15 mph \nrange but could be gusting up to 30 mph.  Still warm southerly flow \nin place so highs will be in the upper 70s to lower 80s. And given \nthat the pocket of warmer air will now be more placed in southern \nCO/KS, would expect a slightly warmer day on Thursday for the OK \nPanhandle,while other areas will be similar to maybe a degree cooler \nthan Wednesday.  \n\nModels continue to track the large closed low off southern CA to the \nnortheast across central NV by Thursday evening.  This will continue \nto draw up that warmer air we are forecasting for the Panhandles on \nThursday, but it also is a more drier track, with most of the \nmoisture being pulled into central and western NM. As we head into \nFriday that low that ejected to the northeast, may leave a weak \ncutoff low to track over southern CA in it's wake.  However the high \nmay take a more positive tilt, but will struggle to budge from a \neasterly track.  This will put the Panhandles in a more questionable \nposition as far as the weekend moisture and just how much we'll \nbenefit from that moisture plume, as it continues to drive mostly \nover central and eastern NM.  As we move through the weekend, we'll \nbe highly focused on precipitation chances for the western \nPanhandles, maybe over the the Amarillo to Guymon line, with a sharp \ndrop off potential to be mostly dry east of that line.  And right \nalong that line is still highly in question.  That being said, \nseveral rounds of training showers and storms with ample PWAT's in \nthe 1.00-1.30\" range will be anchored over the western Panhandles \nfor the weekend.  Saturday night into Sunday is the best chance for \nprecipitation to expand east as a pretty potent upper trough dives \ndown over the central CONUS.  If the stronger colder solution plays \nout we could be looking at overnight lows Monday night down around \nfreezing for much of the Panhandles, except the southeast.  But a \nlot can change from now through the weekend as to the strength and \namplitude of this cold airmass. \n\nWeber",
